-;; ── the two spellings of one member ────────────────────────────────────────
-; A member of a BL namespace is an Erlang atom spelled as it was written, so
-; `(defn hash-key …)` exports `hash-key`. Nothing munges: `slash-target`
-; (compiler.bl:442) passes the member text through literally whether it resolved
-; the prefix to a namespace or to a host module. But the compiler's OWN literal
-; lowers name the runtime with `_` (`:hash_key` at :592, `:invoke` at :199,
-; `:nth` at :1012, `:drop` at :1036, `:splice` at :2406), every `lib/**/*.ex`
-; caller does (`RT.invoke/2`, `RT.hash_key/1`, `RT.seed_core/0`), and the VALUES
-; of the `core` link table do (`"print-str" => :print_str`).
-;
-; So after the cutover the module is named two ways by two conventions that both
-; genuinely exist, and a name it does not export is a call site that breaks at
-; RUNTIME, not at compile time — the link table is consulted on invocation. An
-; alias costs one line and no runtime (it delegates straight to the owner, like
-; the kebab line above it); a missing export costs a broken build.
-;
-; (The deeper fix is to munge at the boundary the way Clojure munges `-` to `_`
-; for the JVM, so there is ONE spelling in source. That is a change to `Emit`
-; with bootstrap-wide blast radius, and it does not belong inside a cutover that
-; has to land green. Noted, not smuggled in.)
